const SITE_INDEX = [{"title": "Colour", "area": "Foundations", "url": "foundations/colour.html", "summary": "Role names for every colour, and how a theme changes the values.", "text": "Colour Chart series Scroll bars  Colour Role names only. Components never use a raw colour, so a theme is a change of values. Every text pair meets WCAG 2.2 AA (4.5:1) in Light, Dark and High contrast. Chart series 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Empty Scroll bars Lena Abery Owen Adair Iris Ancell Priya Arden Tom Ashby Nadia Avery Felix Bannon Ruth Barlow Sam Calder Jordan Carrow Alex Clayburn Hana Dunmore Mara Ellison Corin Fallow Ada Greaves Ivo Lennard Bea Lyle Noor Pemberton Use --color-accent for the one main action, the current page bar and links. --color-text-muted for meta lines and hints, never for the only copy of something important. --status-* pairs only inside chips, notices, stats and edges, always with words. Accent Teal (UX-02, option B); changing it is one token in each theme. Scroll bars Thin and square in every scrolling area, in --color-scrollbar (the border colour; the text colour in High contrast) on a clear track. They stay visible; only shelves hide theirs, behind arrows. Chart series --series-1 to --series-7 in a fixed order that follows the thing, never its rank. Checked for colour blindness, and 3:1 against the surface, in all four themes. --series-empty with a hatch for free space. "}, {"title": "Accent colours", "area": "Foundations", "url": "foundations/accent-colours.html", "summary": "The accent colours and the rules they keep in every theme.", "text": "Accent colours Teal palettes/teal.css Dusk palettes/dusk.css Lime palettes/lime.css Rose palettes/rose.css Amber palettes/amber.css Graphite palettes/graphite.css  Accent colours Six colours. Teal and Graphite are accents over Mica's neutral greys; Dusk, Lime, Rose and Amber also tint the page and cards in Light and Dark, after the Hallmark themes. Load tokens.css , then one palette file, then components.css . The accent changes links, the main button, the chosen item, the count and the logo in every theme. High contrast keeps pure white or black; focus, status colours and chart series never change. Blue, the default, is tokens.css itself; Teal is the colour before Mica. Teal palettes/teal.css The colour before Mica: calm and clear. Your app 3 Light: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Your app 3 Dark: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Dusk palettes/dusk.css Periwinkle on blue slate: quiet evenings, reading. Your app 3 Light: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Your app 3 Dark: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Lime palettes/lime.css Lime on blue-black: sharp and technical. Your app 3 Light: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Your app 3 Dark: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Rose palettes/rose.css Fluorescent pink on warm paper: playful, media. Your app 3 Light: body text and a link . Save Cancel Ready Y"}, {"title": "Type, space and icons", "area": "Foundations", "url": "foundations/type-space-icons.html", "summary": "One typeface, the size and space scales and the icon sizes.", "text": "Type, space and icons Section heading, h2 (1.25rem) Group heading, h3 (1rem, 600)  Type, space and icons One typeface, Atkinson Hyperlegible Next, at five sizes that follow the Text size setting. Space on a 4px scale. Icons from Lucide at 16, 18 and 20px, 2px stroke. Page heading (2rem, rare) Page heading, h1 (1.5rem) Section heading, h2 (1.25rem) Group heading, h3 (1rem, 600) Body text (1rem). The quick brown fox reads three chapters before supper. Small and muted (0.875rem): meta lines, hints and counts. Extra small (0.75rem): chips and tags only. 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48 {{i:book-open}}{{i:compass}}{{i:activity}}{{i:settings}}{{i:server}}{{i:hard-drive}}{{i:download}}{{i:search}}{{i:sun}}{{i:menu}}{{i:x}}{{i:check}}{{i:circle-help}}{{i:help}}{{i:help-new-tab}}{{i:triangle-alert}} Rules Sentence case everywhere. No capitals for emphasis, no all-caps labels. Numbers that change use .numeric (tabular figures). An icon beside a word is decoration ( aria-hidden ); an icon alone needs a name. "}, {"title": "Shell", "area": "Components", "url": "components/shell.html", "summary": "The header and menus, the phone menu, page head, footer, Display, banners, alerts and the command palette.", "text": "Header and menus Phone header and menu Page head Footer Display button Page banner Popover and alerts centre Alerts Command palette  Header and menus Three names: the side menu (the rail on the left), the level 1 menu (header row 1, with search and Display) and the level 2 menu (header row 2, only when the page has one). All three have icons. {{i-lg:search}}Search {{i-lg:book-open}}Library {{i-lg:compass}}Discover {{i:book-open}} My library {{i:bookmark}} Following {{i:list-checks}} Library review 12 to review {{i-lg:search}} {{i-lg:help-new-tab}} Help with this page (opens in a new tab) {{i-lg:sun}} Display {{i:book-copy}} In my libraries {{i:users}} Authors {{i:library}} Series {{i:archive}} All saved titles Use Nothing else goes in the header: page actions live in the page head or the section toolbar. Tabs that do not fit show icons only ( .is-compact ) with the names as tooltips; the 2nd level scrolls sideways. A count on a tab says what it counts in hidden text. Search button Search is always one button away (TheMightyTRexUK, 4 November 2026): .rail__search , first in the rail under the logo, moves focus to the header's search box. On phones, where the box is hidden, .site-header__search-btn in the header opens the same box as a row under the header. Escape or a press outside closes it and returns focus. B"}, {"title": "Actions", "area": "Components", "url": "components/actions.html", "summary": "Buttons and menu buttons.", "text": "Buttons Menu button  Buttons 44px high, square, bold 0.875rem. One primary button per view. Words say what happens: \"Save connection\", not \"OK\". {{i:plus}}Add list Test connection Cancel {{i:trash-2}}Remove Saving… Compact (tables only) {{i:x}} {{i-lg:sun}} Display {{i:ellipsis}} http://example.test:8080 {{i:copy}} {{i:refresh-cw}}Refresh all {{i:chevron-down}} Variants .btn--primary : the one main action. .btn--quiet : Cancel and secondary links. .btn--danger : removing; asks first in a dialog. .btn--compact : 32px, inside dense tables only. Accessibility Icon-only buttons have a name that includes the thing: \"More actions for The Salt Road\". A button that cannot act yet says why beside it, or stays available and explains when pressed. Menu button Related actions in one button instead of a row of them (the Lists menu). Opens with focus on the first item; arrow keys move; Escape closes. {{i:list}}Lists{{i:chevron-down}} {{i:plus}}Add list {{i:refresh-cw}}Refresh all lists Refresh Goodreads lists Refresh StoryGraph lists {{i:arrow-up-down}}Sort: Title{{i:chevron-down}} Sort by Title Author Recently added Use For actions, use menuitem ; for one choice from a list (sort), menuitemradio . For a form value"}, {"title": "Inputs", "area": "Components", "url": "components/inputs.html", "summary": "Fields, choices, filters, combobox, tag input, secret field, schedule and the error summary.", "text": "Fields File picker Checkboxes, switches and choices Sortable list Filter chips and applied filters Combobox (searchable select) Tag input Secret field Schedule picker Error summary  Fields A visible label before every control, a hint under the label when needed, and an error that says how to fix it. Server address The address this app uses to reach qBittorrent. Download category {{i:circle-alert}}Use letters, numbers, - or _ only, with no spaces. Check for new releases Every day Every week Never Minimum free space GB Copies to keep Size of this card Small Medium Wide Notes Kept for the family account. Rules No placeholder as a label. Placeholders only show an example. Errors appear on leaving the field or on save, never while the person is still entering text; focus moves to the first error on save. Units sit beside the box, not in the label. Classes .field , .hint , .field-error (also written .field__error ), .input , .select , .textarea , .input-group , .form-grid .input--narrow : a number field about eight characters wide, so a count does not stretch across the row. .select--compact : a select at the compact control height (32px), for a dense toolbar or a card's tools. File picker The new version's zip file From the plug-in's own page. {{i:upload}}Choose a file No file chosen {{i:upload}}Upload and check Checkboxes, switches and choices Checkboxes for thin"}, {"title": "Content", "area": "Components", "url": "components/content.html", "summary": "Status, tooltips, facts, charts, rows, tables, notices, toasts, callouts, disclosure and the log viewer.", "text": "Status, counts and tags Where status goes Jellyfin Tooltip Facts, stats and meters Sparkline and gauge Rows and tables Tables that line up Pagination Download history Notices, empty and loading No downloads yet Toasts and saved notes Callouts Disclosure, help and steps Version 1.10.0 Version 1.9.0 Log viewer Jellyfin log Drop shadows on and off Shadows off  Status, counts and tags A status chip shows a word, a shape and a colour together, so it works without colour. One vocabulary across both apps (guides/CONTENT-DESIGN.md). {{i:circle-check}}In library {{i:info}}Downloading {{i:triangle-alert}}Needs review {{i:circle-x}}Failed {{i:minus}}Paused {{i:clock}}Wanted Library review 12 to review Audiobook Unabridged AL J v1.9.0 Upgrade available App version v1.9.0 , update available: version 1.10.0 App version v1.9.0 Where status goes Jellyfin {{i:circle-check}}Up to date Version 10.11.2 Live TV 42 channels Sonarr {{i:circle-check}} Connected 127.0.0.1:8989 SABnzbd {{i:circle-x}} Not connected 127.0.0.1:8085 Shapes Success, info and neutral: a dot. Warning: a diamond. Danger: a square. Muted: a ring. High contrast adds a border to every chip. Chip style Outline with an icon (style B, chosen on the UX-04 board). Where status goes A heading: .title-status , chips straight after the name, centred on its line. Never on a line of their own under it. Details such as a version go on the right. A list of things with a state: .status-icon straight after the name, the word in hidden text and a tooltip. A row with actions: the chip in .row__side . A"}, {"title": "Panels, cards and tabs", "area": "Components", "url": "components/containers.html", "summary": "Panels and cards, and tabs.", "text": "Panels and cards Jellyfin Disk 3 · 4 TB Reelix Card band Network Tabs  Panels and cards A panel groups content. A card is one thing that opens its slide-out or page; the whole card is the target, and its other buttons sit on top of it. J Jellyfin Films, series and music. {{i:circle-check}}Running Updated 2 days ago {{i:hard-drive}} Disk 3 · 4 TB Used 3.1 of 3.6 TB {{i:triangle-alert}}Needs attention R Reelix Films and shows. {{i:copy}} example.test:8000 Card band A grey band across the top of a card with the logo, the name as the heading and a status chip at the right (Starboard's plug-in cards, DASH-428), and a lighter grey footer, .card-foot , with when it last updated. Without a logo, the initials on a tile. Direct play Status: Converting The Expanse, season 2, episode 4 Adding a Roku audio track Updated 30 seconds ago WS Watch statistics , This week 42 hours watched this week Updated 1 minute ago Network Checked 1 minute ago Outside address reachable VPN {{i:circle-check}}Connected Tabs Real tabs for the parts of one item's page (DESIGN-SYSTEM.md section 5): one Tab stop for the tabs, then the arrow keys. Choosing a tab shows its part in place; the address does not change. {{i:book-open}}Summary {{i:book-copy}}Editions 3 editions {{i:folder}}File"}, {"title": "Covers, media and shelves", "area": "Components", "url": "components/media.html", "summary": "Covers and media cards, the A to Z list, load more, series header and shelf.", "text": "Covers and media cards A to Z jump list # A B Load more as you scroll Series header and logo Shelf For you  Covers and media cards Covers at 2:3; audiobook squares are letterboxed, not cropped. Small, Medium and Large come from the account's cover size setting. The Glass Harbour The Glass Harbour Mara Ellison · Book 2 Salt Roads Salt Roads J. Okafor No cover An Unknown Book Unknown author Square audio Audiobook Night Ferry Read by Sam Oduya Behaviour Pressing a book opens its summary slide-out; the full page is a link inside it. The focus ring sits on the cover, so it is visible on any artwork. A to Z jump list Over a long wall of covers: # for titles that start with a number, then A to Z, from the sort title (\"The Expanse\" is under E, \"Émile\" under E). Letters with nothing are shown but cannot be chosen, and say so to screen readers. It sticks under the header; the letter in view is marked with a bar. One Tab stop: Arrow keys, Home and End move, Enter jumps and focus moves to the letter's heading. On phones the row scrolls sideways. Classes: .az-jump , .az-jump__letter , [aria-disabled=\"true\"] , .wall-letter . Behaviour: SharedComponents.azLetter(title) , azJump(nav, letters) , azGo(nav, letter) and the az-jump event for a wall that shows 40 at a time. # A B C D E F G H I J K L M N O P"}, {"title": "Settings and toolbars", "area": "Components", "url": "components/settings-and-tools.html", "summary": "The section toolbar, settings rows, the save bar, settings patterns, the folder picker, timeline and calendar.", "text": "Section toolbar Settings rows and save bar Download clients Settings patterns Download hours Remove Folder picker Choose a place Choose a folder Timeline Calendar month  Section toolbar Design D: everything that changes what the list shows, on one line under the heading. The same on every list in both apps. View {{i:layout-grid}} Covers {{i:list}} List Audiobooks Ebooks 2,418 books {{i:arrow-up-down}}Sort: Title{{i:chevron-down}} Title Author Settings rows and save bar Label and explanation on the left, the control on the right. Things with several fields (a connection, an account) open a slide-out to edit, so the list stays in view. Download clients Where requested books are sent to download. Check connections How often to test each client in the background. Every 5 minutes Every hour Remove finished downloads After they are imported and have seeded to the ratio set in the client. Remove finished downloads qBittorrent Category starboard · {{i:circle-check}}Connected {{i:pencil}}Edit qBittorrent 2 unsaved changes Save changes Discard Settings patterns Every Settings page uses only these nine: section, setting rows, choice cards, location, form with Save, status line, empty state, lists that open and the danger zone (DESIGN-SYSTEM.md, section 9). Download hours Active When the download apps may run, so the server stays quick in the evening. Only at"}, {"title": "Slide-outs and dialogs", "area": "Components", "url": "components/slide-outs-and-dialogs.html", "summary": "The slide-out and the dialog.", "text": "Slide-out Dialog Remove qBittorrent?  Slide-out From the right, over a dimmed page. For the details of one thing, and for editing it, without losing your place in the list. Its header wears the page background ( --color-slide-header ) and its body is the raised surface: white in Light, the dark surface in Dark. The phone menu, which slides in from the start edge, has the same design. {{i:sun}}Display and accessibility Book summary Edit a connection App or disk details Download that needs attention Behaviour Focus moves to Close; Tab stays inside; Escape, Close or a press outside closes it; focus returns to what opened it. The page behind does not scroll. Its address can include the open item, so Back closes it. Editing: Save and Cancel in a footer that stays put; Remove on the far right, which asks first. Sizes 420px; .side-panel--wide 640px for logs or long forms; full width on phones. Colour Header: the page background, so it reads as part of the page. Body and footer: the raised surface. Dialogs keep the plain header, so a slide-out and a dialog are told apart at a glance. High contrast keeps a solid line under the header. Classes .side-panel-layer , .side-panel-backdrop , .side-panel , .panel-header , .side-panel_"}, {"title": "App patterns", "area": "Components", "url": "components/app-patterns.html", "summary": "Starboard's own patterns: page topics, the update list, places and service logos, the dashboard, show once, a wizard in a slide-out and folding sections.", "text": "Page topics Names and apps Appearance Download hours Alerts Update list Places and service logos Google Drive Disk 2 Add a place Dashboard grid and cards Storage Watch statistics On TV now Quick links Show once Wizard in a slide-out Folding sections with tables {{i:chevron-down}}Channels3 of 3 on {{i:chevron-right}}Ad breaks12 breaks  Page topics A long page of settings cut into topics, one shown at a time. The tabs come from the page's own sections, so a page with four or more sections gets them and a short one does not. It is not Tabs , which show the parts of one thing in one panel. Names and apps Appearance Download hours Alerts Names and apps The name of this server, and the apps on it. Appearance Theme, text size and spacing. Download hours When downloads may run. Alerts What Starboard tells you, and how. Rules Four or more sections make tabs; a page may ask for fewer. Each tab is named by its section's heading. Left and Right move between tabs, Home and End go to the first and last. Only the selected tab is in the Tab order. A tab's address ( #topic ) opens it. Tabs wrap onto a second line and never scroll, so every topic is in view. From 768px wide and 640px tall the tabs stay under the header; --topic-tabs-h (set by the page's script) is their height, so the page keeps what has focus clear of them. Classes .topic-tabs , .topic-tabs__tab , .topic-off , .settings-sections (one gap of 32px; 40px on a .page--settings page), .page--settings (full width, text no wider than 75 characters). Styles-only part pa"}, {"title": "Choosing", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/choosing.html", "summary": "Slide-out, dialog or page, and which item to use.", "text": "Slide-out, dialog or page Which item to use  Slide-out, dialog or page The rule both apps follow when something opens. Use When Examples Slide-out Details of one item in a list, or editing it, where keeping the list in view helps. Can hold a few actions. Also settings people change while the page stays in view. Book summary; a request in the queue; a download client, indexer or reading account; a user; a log entry; an app, disk or download on Starboard; a backup run; Release notes; Display and accessibility. Dialog A decision or a short task that must be finished or cancelled before going on. One or two fields at most. Remove or reset (confirm); Add list; a password check before a download or restore. Page Something people come back to, link to or spend time in; long content with its own sections. Book, author and series pages; narrator page; Disk health; Settings sections; Upgrade wizard. In place A small change to a value already on the page. Switches and selects in settings rows; filter chips; cover size. Which item to use Pick by what the thing is, not by its appearance. The rules are in DESIGN-SYSTEM.md, section 10 (Starboard DASH-309). Item Use it for Not for List rows A list of things that each have a state or an acti"}, {"title": "Wizard", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/wizard.html", "summary": "Every job done in steps: the pieces, and signing in to a service.", "text": "Wizard Choose a Usenet app What happens next Decision tree What is Usenet? What you need What this step does Wizard pieces Signing in to an outside service  Wizard Every job done in steps: first-run set-up, upgrades, restores and migrations. The rules are in docs/WIZARD.md . Beside the step are two review aids, What happens next and the decision tree: they are for testing only, shown while an app's testing setting is on, never in the finished product. New install · step 7 of 18 · Build your media server 11 to go Torrent , done Usenet , current part Download options Choose a Usenet app The Usenet app fetches what the app finds, from your Usenet provider. Usenet app SABnzbd (recommended) The one most guides use. Sets itself up from your provider's details. NZBGet Uses less memory on a small server. I do not use Usenet Leaves out the Usenet sites to search. After it is installed, SABnzbd is at http://192.0.2.10:8080{{i:external-link}} (opens in a new tab) {{i:copy}} docker compose up -d sabnzbd {{i:copy}} Copy {{i:help}} What is Usenet? {{i:help}} What is SABnzbd? {{i:help}} Help with this step Continue to Download options Back Skip this step Review aids, for testing only. Not in the finished product. What happens next If: SABnzbd Your choice Next you enter your Usenet provider's details, and SABnzbd is installed with them. If: NZBGet Th"}, {"title": "Terminal", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/terminal.html", "summary": "How a wizard appears and behaves in a terminal.", "text": "Terminal A question A job's bar Help, a box over the step Plain mode, for screen readers Feedback as a QR code (80 by 30)  Terminal The same wizard drawn in a terminal ( docs/TERMINAL.md ; the code is in terminal/ ). Each drawing is 80 columns, the usual window. A question ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ App | Your home network step 1 of 4 │ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░ 0% Can phones and other computers on your home network open App? ● 1 Yes, with your password (recommended) Phones, tablets and other computers at home can open it. 2 No, only this computer You can change this later in Settings, Security. › Your choice [1]: ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── b Back ? Help f Feedback q Stop Enter: recommended A job's bar ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ App | Install what is missing step 3 of 6 │ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ ███████████████████████░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░ 33% Installing (2 of 3)… ✔ Downloading the installer ██████████████▓▓▒▒▒▒▓▓▒▒░░░░░░░░░░░░░░"}, {"title": "Lists and changes", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/lists-and-changes.html", "summary": "The event list, what will change, earlier and later, and recent checks.", "text": "Event list What will change Earlier and later Recent checks  Event list Things that happened, newest first, each with its time and a status icon (the word is hidden text and a tooltip). Used for disk events on Starboard. For the history of one thing with steps, use the Timeline. {{i:circle-x}} Problem: 2026-11-02 09:15 /dev/sdc: SMART self-test failed {{i:triangle-alert}} Warning: 2026-11-01 03:00 /dev/sdb: reallocated sectors 0 to 8 {{i:info}} Information: 2026-10-31 03:00 /dev/sda: extended self-test completed Classes .rows , .row--no-lead , .status-icon[data-tone] , .numeric What will change Before anything is copied or changed in another app, a table says what happens to each thing, with one chip per row. Then one primary button does it. Used for Copy app settings on Starboard. Download clients Name What happens qBittorrent (qBittorrent) {{i:info}}Will be added SABnzbd (SABnzbd) {{i:minus}}Already there Naming {{i:triangle-alert}}Will be replaced {{i:copy}}Copy Words Will be added, Already there (left as it is), Will be replaced. Nothing already there is changed unless the row says Will be replaced. Earlier and later Moving a calendar a month at a time. The buttons name the month they go to; Back to now appears only away from the curren"}, {"title": "Help", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/help.html", "summary": "The help article, the help centre and what is new.", "text": "Help article Before you begin Steps Things to note Find out more The steps at a glance Step 1: Back up your settings Help centre Top links Side tree Journey Your server, step by step 1 · Set up 2 · Use every day 3 · Fix things Flow chart and answer cards Feature card, solver card and version badge Problem solver Plug-in card and band What is new Know when a disk is failing Downloads now show time left  Help article Help topics, guides and release notes shown in an app use .prose . The layouts and rules are in docs/HELP-WRITING.md . A topic does one task; a guide is mostly links to topics. Help centre · Backup and restore Back up your settings How-to Save a copy of Starboard's settings, so you can put them back after a new install. It takes about a minute. Before you begin Sign in as an administrator. Read Choose who can open Starboard . Steps Go to Settings , then Backup . Select Back up now . A message says when the copy is saved. Select Download the backup (ZIP) to keep a copy on another computer. {{i:sparkles}} Tip: Activate 'Back up every night' and Starboard keeps the last 7 copies for you. Things to note A backup holds settings only. Films, music and books stay on their disks and are not copied. To copy those too, read Back up the server . Find out more Article What it covers Restore a saved copy Putting a backup back Move Starboard to a new computer The whole move, step by step Help centre · Guides Move Starboard to a new computer For anyone replacing their server. At the end, Starboard runs on the new computer with all your apps and settings. Allow about an hour. The ste"}, {"title": "Dates", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/dates.html", "summary": "Retiring something, dated changes and time ago.", "text": "Retiring something and dated changes Quiet hours for downloads Time ago  Retiring something and dated changes A removal is not news: say what is going, the date and what to do instead, in the app until the date passes. A change with an agreed date gets a From tag under its title; leave the tag off once the change is live. {{i:triangle-alert}} Limitation: Classic layout ends on 1 December 2026 Shelf layout replaces it, and your settings move across. Read Switch to Shelf layout . Quiet hours for downloads From 1 December 2026 Time ago Relative words on screen, the exact time for screen readers and in the markup. Updated 10 seconds ago , at 16:31 on 3 November 2026 Markup <time datetime=\"2026-11-03T16:31:00\"> with the words, and the exact time in .visually-hidden . "}, {"title": "Icons", "area": "Foundations", "url": "foundations/icons.html", "summary": "Every icon in the sprite.", "text": "activity app-window archive archive-restore arrow-down arrow-left arrow-up arrow-up-down award ban ban bell book book-copy book-marked book-open book-open-text bookmark bookmark bookmark-plus calendar calendar-clock calendar-days chart-line check check chevron-down chevron-left chevron-right circle circle-alert circle-arrow-up circle-check circle-check circle-help circle-x circle-x clock clock cloud compass copy cpu database download download ellipsis external-link eye eye-off file-pen file-question file-search file-text file-x film filter folder folder-input folder-open gauge globe grip-vertical hard-drive hard-drive-download headphones heart-pulse help help-new-tab history hourglass house import inbox info info key-round layers layout-grid library link link-2 list list-checks list-ordered list-plus lock log-out menu mic minus monitor music network octagon-x panel-right-open pause pencil play plug plus puzzle radar radio refresh-cw rotate-ccw scroll-text search search send server server-cog settings shield-check sliders-horizontal sparkles square-check star sun tags terminal thermometer timer trash-2 triangle-alert triangle-alert tv undo-2 undo-2 upload user user-plus user-round users wifi wrench x zap"}, {"title": "Design system", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/design-system.html", "summary": "Design guide", "text": " Version 0.2, 26 November 2026. The design system of Starboard, its plug-ins and Stallboard (it began in November 2026 as Storyarr's, which was retired on 11 November 2026). The target every page moves towards. COMPONENTS.md lists what exists today, and the redesign plan behind it is kept privately. Other guides cover other things: - words are in CONTENT-DESIGN.md , and changelogs are in CHANGELOGS.md - accessibility is in ACCESSIBILITY.md , WCAG.md and ALT-TEXT.md - security checks, and how to report them, are in SECURITY-CHECKS.md - the component reference is LIBRARY.md For AI agents: when you build or touch UI, use these tokens, templates and components. If something you need is missing, add it here first (and say so to TheMightyTRexUK) instead of inventing a one-off style. 1. Principles One way to do each job. One status chip, one card, one side panel, one dialog, one toolbar. The same place every time. Page title, section navigation, toolbar, content, side panel: same order on every page. Status first. Every book shows its status with the same words, colour and icon wherever it appears. Calm by default. Light theme, plenty of space, covers carry the colour; the interface stays quiet. Mica (owner, 18 November 2026, DASH-479). Mica has calm neutral greys and 6px corners ( --radius ) on buttons, chips, cards, inputs, menus and dialogs. Cards have a light layered lift. There is one accent colour, which is Blue unless another is chosen. The dashboard card is the model for every container. It has a surface, an edge in --color-divider (the same colour as every rule), Mica's corners, a grey band across the top for its name and status, and its body under it. Avatars, spinners and status dots stay round. The design before Mica is the Classic style in Starboard's Styles plug-in. 6. Accessible is the baseline. WCAG 2.2 AA in every theme; 44px controls; keyboard first. 7. Inspired, not copied. Audiobookshelf and Radarr are references for ideas only; no copied code or assets. 2. Tokens One token file ( apps/web/src/tokens.css , task UX-02) with role names , defined once per theme (Light, Dark, High contrast). Components use only these. Colour roles Token Use --color-bg Page background --color-surface Panels, cards, header, side panel --color-surface-raised Dialogs, menus, toasts --color-surface-sunken Wells, code, empty cover placeholder --color-text Body text --color-text-muted Secondary text (still 4.5:1) --color-border Dividers and control borders (3:1 for contr"}, {"title": "Component library", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/library.html", "summary": "Design guide", "text": " Version 1, 2 November 2026. One component library for every app, so they share one design and one behaviour. It follows DESIGN-SYSTEM.md and the guides in this folder ( CONTENT-DESIGN.md , HELP-WRITING.md and ACCESSIBILITY.md ). File names in the tables in this guide are relative to the root of the repository. Open components/index.html in a browser : every component in it works as it does in the apps. The component pages use the same header and menus as the apps. The main menu and search are in row 1, and the component groups are in row 2. Display opens the Display and accessibility slide-out, with theme, text size, reduced motion, high contrast, underlined links and wider spacing. Slide-outs, menus, the phone menu and the dialog all work in the gallery. Files File What it is tokens.css Every colour, size, space and motion value, as role names, for Light, Dark, High contrast and High contrast dark, plus the display preference classes components.css Every component, in @layer shared , using only the tokens components.js The behaviour every component must have (menus, slide-outs, dialogs, phone menu, filter chips, copy, help, display settings), in plain JavaScript icons.svg Lucide icons as an SVG sprite ( <use href=\"#i-book-open\"> ); tools/icons.txt lists them fonts/ Atkinson Hyperlegible Next (OFL licence) gallery.src.html The source of the component pages in components/ , foundations/ and patterns/ (edit it, then run python3 tools/site.py ) contrast-report.txt Made by tools/contrast.py (not committed): measured contrast for every text and control pair in all four themes; all pass WCAG 2.2 AA accent-report.txt Made by tools/check_accents.py --report accent-report.txt : every token pair the components draw, for every accent colour, in all four themes, including the menu hover fill and its edge; all pass WCAG 2.2 AA areas/ Design boards for Starboard's screens, using the library, with slide-outs where they fit tools/build.py Turns an icon name written in double braces, as in i, a colon and the name, into the icon, and <!--ICONS--> into the sprite for any *.src.html tools/icons.py Rebuilds icons.svg from Lucide (the lucide-react package; set its folder in tools/paths.local.json ) service-logos.svg The brand logos for places (Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, Backblaze B2, Amazon S3, WebDAV): artwork from simpleicons.org, CC0; each stays its owner's trademark, shown to identify that service only tools/sync.py , tools/paths.py Copies the library into Starboard,"}, {"title": "Components by area", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/components.html", "summary": "Design guide", "text": " Updated 20 November 2026. Every component in the shared library, by the area it belongs to, as the component pages ( components/index.html ) group them. LIBRARY.md is the reference, with each component's classes, behaviour and rules. When you add a component, add it here and in LIBRARY.md . Area Components Foundations Colour roles and status tones, accent palettes, type, space and size, icons, utilities ( .visually-hidden , .stack-* , .numeric ) Shell Side menu (rail), header and its level 1 and level 2 menus ( .menu-tabs ), phone menu, page and page head, footer, Display button, page banner, popover, alerts centre, command palette Actions Button, icon button, split button, menu button and menu, copy button, action row, action list Inputs Field, text box, select and text area, search field, input with unit, checkbox and radio, switch, segmented control, option cards, filter chips and applied filters, sortable list, form layout, file picker, folder picker, combobox, tag input, secret field, schedule picker, error summary and field errors, range Content Status chip, title with status, status icon, tooltip, count and update dot, tag, avatar and app icon, cover, facts, stat, sparkline and gauge, progress and meter, waiting mark and busy button, rows, table, tables that line up, pagination, notice and callout, empty state, skeleton and loading line, toast, saved note, help, disclosure, releases, steps, command, line chart, log viewer, load more as you scroll, prose, time ago Containers Panel, card and card band, media card and grid, A to Z jump list, section toolbar, save bar, settings rows and section group, timeline, list and detail, item header, series header, shelf, calendar month, selection bar, tabs, slide-out, dialog, panel header, back link and breadcrumbs Patterns Slide-out, dialog or page; which item to use ( DESIGN-SYSTEM.md section 10); wizard and its pieces ( WIZARD.md ); signing in to a service; restart where it is needed; terminal ( TERMINAL.md ) Help centre Top links, side tree, journey, flow chart, answer card, feature card, solver card and solver, version badge, plug-in card and band ( HELP-WRITING.md section 11) Some class names differ from the component's name: - .disclosure is the accordion, .side-panel the slide-out and .segmented the segmented control. - .steps and .wizard are the step list and wizard, and .shelf is the row that scrolls sideways. - .count , .chip and .tag are the number, status and kind labels. - .range is the slider, .f"}, {"title": "Wizard", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/wizard.html", "summary": "Patterns guide", "text": " 4 November 2026. One set of rules for every wizard in every app that uses the library. That covers first-run set-up, upgrades, restores, migrations and any other job done in steps. The classes are in components.css and the behaviour is in components.js . A live example is in the gallery, section Wizard. A wizard in a terminal follows TERMINAL.md as well. A wizard is a page, not a dialog. People link to it, come back to it and spend time in it (\"Slide-out, dialog or page\" in LIBRARY.md ). 1. Shape Only what the app can show. A wizard runs inside the app, so it starts once the app is installed and running. Anything needed before that (Docker, a folder, running the installer) belongs on the help page and in the installer. The installer checks it and stops with what to do. Never ask in the wizard about something the wizard could not run without (4 November 2026). A mock-up that shows those earlier steps marks them off as \"Before the wizard\", with their own count. The wizard's progress starts at its own first step. Part Where Classes Progress bar Top of the wizard .wizard-progress (a .meter with a .progress bar) Step bar for a step in parts Under the progress bar, only on a step in parts .steps.steps--sub The step Under them .wizard , with .wizard__foot for the buttons Help At the end of the part of the step it explains .help-links with .help-link buttons, opening a slide-out ( .side-panel ) Review aids, testing only Beside the step on computers, under it on phones .wizard-aside holding .wizard-next (What happens next) and .wizard-tree (decision tree) Review aids are for testing, never the finished product (TheMightyTRexUK, 4 November 2026). What happens next and the decision tree help people who review a wizard follow where each choice leads. An app shows them only while its testing setting is active (the same setting as its Send feedback button, Snaglist ), and mock-ups show them. Leave .wizard-aside out of the page, or give it hidden , and the step takes the whole width. .wizard-layout puts the step on the left and the review aids on the right from 1100px, one under the other under that width. The step always comes first in the reading order. 2. The first question Ask which job first, when there is more than one. For a set-up, the jobs are \"New install\", \"Restore a backup\" and \"Migrate from other apps\". Each answer has its own wizard, with its own steps and count. .wizard-paths shows each job as a card with its stages and number of steps, and marks the chos"}, {"title": "Terminal", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/terminal.html", "summary": "Patterns guide", "text": " 5 November 2026. The same jobs as WIZARD.md , for a wizard that runs in a terminal. That means a server with no desktop, reached over SSH, with no browser to finish in. Starboard's ./install.sh --terminal is the first one. Every rule in WIZARD.md about questions, words and help still applies. This guide says how each appears in a terminal. 1. Two ways to draw it, one set of words Boxed is the default in a terminal at least 60 characters wide and 20 lines tall. Each step starts on a cleared screen, with a heading box, a progress bar and a menu fixed on the bottom line. Plain applies with an APP_PLAIN=1 setting such as STARBOARD_PLAIN=1 , a --plain option, a smaller window, TERM=dumb , or output that is not a terminal. It gives numbered lines that scroll, with nothing cleared, redrawn or drawn over. Screen readers lose their place when a screen is redrawn, and they read box characters aloud. So plain mode is the one for them. Say how to choose it in the help. - Both ask the same questions in the same order, with the same answers. Only the drawing changes. - The browser set-up too. The terminal wizard asks the same questions, in the same order, with the same answers and help as the app's browser wizard. A change to one is made to the other in the same change ( WIZARD.md section 2, One wizard, two ways to show it). - NO_COLOR keeps the boxes and drops the colour. 2. Fit the window, and keep the frame in place The frame never moves. The heading box and the progress bar stay at the top, and the menu stays on the bottom two lines, at all times. Only the lines between them scroll (a scrolling area set between the two). A long list, a card or a message never pushes the heading or the menu out of view. Measure the window at every step, and again when it changes size (SIGWINCH). Draw the step again to fit. Draw the frame, then the step's last lines, then the question that is waiting, so the person can carry on entering the answer. A column, centred. The frame, text and menu sit in a column at most 100 characters wide. In a wider window the column is centred, with equal space either side; at 60 to 100 columns it is the whole window. The column is worked out again at every step and every change of size, and the waiting question moves with it. Text wraps between words at the column's width, and is wrapped again when the window changes size, never cut mid-word. Under 60 columns or 20 lines, use the plain way (section 1). A long list in a short window shows what fits, th"}, {"title": "Content design", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/content-design.html", "summary": "Words guide", "text": " Version 1.6, 26 November 2026. How every word in Starboard, its plug-ins and Stallboard is written: page titles, menus, buttons, settings, messages, release notes, help and the README. It applies to new text and to anything you touch. Help articles also follow HELP-WRITING.md ; changelogs also follow CHANGELOGS.md . About this guide What it is This is a content design system, not only a style guide. A style guide is a set of rules written by writers, for writers. A content design system adds ready-made patterns for the moments that come up again and again: an empty page, an error, a confirmation, a setting. With a pattern to start from, anyone can make a sound decision about words without starting from a blank page. It sits beside the component library in this repository, so words are designed with the components rather than added at the end. It has four layers: Layer What it covers Where Voice How every app sound Section 1 Rules Plain English, word choices, punctuation, links and statuses Sections 2 to 6 Patterns Ready-made words for common moments Section 7 Checks Accessibility in content, and the checklist Sections 8 and 9 Who it is for Anyone who puts words in front of the people who use Starboard or Stallboard: developers writing a button or an error, designers making a board, people writing help or release notes, and AI assistants doing any of these. It is public, so you may also use it in your own projects under the guide licence (CC BY 4.0). How to use it Read section 1 once, so you know how the apps sound. Before you write something new, find its pattern in section 7 and start from that. Keep sections 2 and 5 open while you write: the rules, and the words each app uses. Before you finish, go through the checklist in section 9. The gallery, the tools and the other guides are listed in Design resources and tools . Propose a change Open an issue or a pull request on the design system repository . Name the section and the rule you want to add, change or remove, and give the words before and after. The guide is reviewed after any release that changes words in either app. How to make and test a change is in Make a change . For AI agents: treat every rule marked Rule as a check that must pass before a change is finished. Section 9 is the checklist. When a rule and an on-screen label disagree, copy the label exactly (rule 2.11). To get an AI assistant to write instructions, and to check what it writes, follow HELP-WRITING.md section 9. 1. Voice and tone "}, {"title": "Help writing", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/help-writing.html", "summary": "Words guide", "text": " Version 1.4, 17 November 2026. How help is written for every help centre (Starboard, Snaglist, the plug-in collection and any later one) and any help shown inside the apps. Section 11 is the help centre standard: the layout, the menus, flow charts, the journey, the problem solver and plug-in mode. Every word also follows CONTENT-DESIGN.md . About this guide What it is for Help answers the question someone has at the moment they have it. This guide sets out how that help is organised and laid out. Every app's help centre then works in the same way, with the same three tiers, kinds of topic, layouts and checks. Someone who has used one help centre already knows how the next one is organised. Readers may know very little about computers. Write so that someone who has never opened a terminal can follow every step, and link to the basics for anything they might not know. Who it is for Anyone who writes or checks help: the help centre in an app's repository, help shown inside an app, and README files that teach. That includes AI assistants: section 9 says what to give one and how to check what it writes. It is public, so you may also use it for your own projects under the guide licence (CC BY 4.0). How to use it Choose the tier (section 1): a topic, a guide or the basics. For a topic, choose its one kind (section 2) and the area it belongs to (section 3). Copy the layout for that tier (sections 4 to 6) and write to the rules in section 7. Add screenshots only where section 8 says they help. Before you finish, go through the checklist in section 10 and the one in CONTENT-DESIGN.md . Working examples: the Starboard help centre and the Snaglist help centre . The gallery, the tools and the other guides are listed in Design resources and tools . 1. The three tiers Tier Folder What it is Answers 1 topics/ A topic: one task, one question, one article \"How do I do this one thing?\" 2 guides/ A guide: an ordered journey made of topics \"How do I get from nothing to this outcome?\" 3 basics/ The basics: an idea explained from the start \"What is this, and why does it matter?\" A topic covers one task. A second task is a second topic, and the two link to each other. A guide is mostly links. Each step is one or two sentences and a link to the topic that holds the instructions. A guide never repeats a topic's steps, so each instruction is kept in one place. If a step has no topic, write the topic first. Basics teach, topics and guides direct. If a topic needs to explain what Doc"}, {"title": "Changelogs", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/changelogs.html", "summary": "Words guide", "text": " Version 1.1, 26 November 2026. How every project that uses this design system keeps its changelog: Starboard, Stallboard, Snaglist and the design system itself. It follows the principles of Keep a Changelog 1.1.0 , written in our own words, with dates and wording from CONTENT-DESIGN.md . Release notes shown in an app follow the same change types ( CONTENT-DESIGN.md , section 7). For AI agents: treat every rule marked Rule as a check that must pass before a release is finished. Section 8 is the checklist. 1. What a changelog is for A changelog is a file that lists the notable changes in each version of a project, newest first. People who use the project can then tell what is different before they upgrade. 1.1 Rule: changelogs are for people. Write for the person who runs the app or uses the library, not for the developer who made the change. Say what someone can now do, what works differently and what was fixed. The words follow CONTENT-DESIGN.md : British English, sentence case, no em dashes, no contractions. 1.2 Rule: never dump a commit log. Commits record the steps of the work; a changelog records the difference those steps made. Leave out merge commits, refactoring, test-only changes, formatting and version bumps. One entry often covers several commits, and one commit may not be worth an entry. 1.3 Rule: keep it complete. A changelog with gaps misleads, because people trust it as the record. Small changes may be left out, but every new feature, every change in behaviour, every removal and every security fix goes in. 1.4 Rule: one file, CHANGELOG.md , at the root of the repository, so people find it where they expect it. A project may also publish each release's notes (on GitHub or in the app), made from the same entries. 2. The file # Changelog All notable changes to Starboard are listed here, newest first. The format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), with dates written as in the TRex Design System, and versions follow [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/). ## [Unreleased] ### Added - **Set up in the terminal.** `./install.sh --terminal` asks the set-up questions with no browser. ## [0.7.7] (4 November 2026) Lighter, from the performance review. ### Changed - **Pages open faster.** A page and its files share one connection. ### Security - **Error answers.** Every answer carries the same security headers. [Unreleased]: https://github.com/TheMightyTRex/starboard/compare/v0.7.7...HEAD [0.7.7]: https://github.com/TheMi"}, {"title": "Alt text", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/alt-text.html", "summary": "Words guide", "text": " Version 1.0, 4 November 2026. This guide says how to describe images in Starboard, Stallboard, their help and this design system. It follows the principles in the BBC's How to write text descriptions (alt text) . Every word follows CONTENT-DESIGN.md . The examples are made up, as CONTENT-DESIGN.md rule 2.23 asks. About this guide What it is for A text description, also called alt text, says in words what an image shows or does. Screen readers read it aloud and braille displays show it. It also appears when an image does not load, lets voice control users name an image link, and helps search engines. For many people it is not an alternative: it is the image. WCAG 2.2 success criterion 1.1.1 requires a text alternative for every image ( WCAG.md section 2). Who it is for Anyone who adds an image to a page, a board, a help topic, a release note or a README, and anyone who reviews one. How to use it Decide what kind of image it is (section 2). Write the description for that kind (sections 3 to 7), and for people (section 8). Go through the checklist in section 9. 1. Principles Context decides. There is no right length or set of words. The same image needs a different description on different pages, so never copy a description from another page without rereading it. Say what matters here. Ask why the image is on this page and what it tells the reader, then say that. Imagine telling someone on the phone. Describe the image, in plain words, to someone who does not have it in front of them. Short, then the page. Aim for about 150 characters. If an image needs more, put the detail in the page's own text, a list or a table, and keep the description short. Some screen readers read a description in one go, with no way to go back. No \"image of\". Screen readers already say it is an image. Name the style when it matters: \"Screenshot of…\", \"Illustration of…\", \"Diagram of…\". Nothing the page already says. If the text beside the image gives the facts, the description can be shorter. Never leave it out. An image with no alt makes a screen reader read the file name. An image that adds nothing gets an empty description, alt=\"\" , so it is skipped. 2. The five kinds of image Kind What it is What its description does Decorative Adds nothing to the meaning: a border, a background pattern, an icon beside its own words Empty: alt=\"\" , or aria-hidden=\"true\" on an SVG Informative Shows something the reader needs, or sets the scene Says what it shows, in this page's context Functional "}, {"title": "Screenshots", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/screenshots.html", "summary": "Words guide", "text": " How screenshots in help are chosen, taken, marked up and described, for every app that uses this design system. The rules are written so a person or an AI tool can follow them without guessing. Section 9 is a short version to give an AI tool with its task. About this guide What it is for A screenshot is there to carry a step the reader could not follow from the words alone. These rules keep every screenshot accurate, safe to publish and readable with a screen reader, and make the pictures in every app's help match. Who it is for Anyone adding or replacing a screenshot in an app's help, by hand or with an AI tool. It sits with HELP-WRITING.md (section 8 points here), ALT-TEXT.md (text descriptions) and CONTENT-DESIGN.md (rules 2.20 and 2.23). How to use it Write the steps first ( HELP-WRITING.md ). Decide which steps need a picture (section 1). Take it from made-up data (section 2), after the page has settled (section 3). Mark it up (sections 4 and 5), check it (section 7), and describe it (section 6). 1. When a screenshot earns its place An app may set a minimum, and Starboard's is 3 in every topic (tier one, owner, 7 November 2026). Choose them with the test in this section. Pick the steps a reader could least follow from the words, the page they start on, and what appears when it has worked. Beyond the minimum, test each picture. Does it carry a step the reader could not otherwise follow? If not, leave the step as text. - Worth a picture: a control with no words on it (a menu of three dots, an icon button), a dialog the reader has not met before, or a page where the control is hard to find. - Rarely worth a picture: a menu that is the same on every page, a second table repeating the one before it, or a control the step already names in bold, such as \"Select Save\". - Split rather than crowd. An article that needs ten pictures is two articles; split it where the task has a natural break, losing no step. - When you finish, say which steps you left as text on purpose. 2. Data: only made-up data, never real data Take screenshots from a test copy of the app with made-up data (rule 2.23 in CONTENT-DESIGN.md ): example names, .example addresses, 07700 900 numbers. Never from a real server, a real account or anyone's own library. Where a page can only be reached with real data, mask it before capturing. Replace names, addresses, numbers and messages with made-up values in the page itself, then add a visible note: \"Example data\". Repeat the note in the text descr"}, {"title": "Accessibility", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/accessibility.html", "summary": "Accessibility and security guide", "text": " Version 1.2, 4 November 2026. For Starboard, its plug-ins and Stallboard. The floor is WCAG 2.2 level AA on every page, in every theme (Light, Dark, High contrast and High contrast dark) and every layout. Content rules are in CONTENT-DESIGN.md section 8. WCAG.md says how each WCAG point is met and what each app must still do. ALT-TEXT.md says how to write text descriptions for images. For AI agents: every item marked Check must pass before a UI change is finished. Section 6 says how to test each one. 1. Display and accessibility Every page of every app, Sign in and Set up included, has the Display button as the last item in the header. It opens the Display and accessibility slide-out (gallery: Display button). The choices, in this order: Setting Values Default Theme Match device, Light, Dark Light (owner, 18 November 2026) Text size 100%, 125%, 150%, 175% 100% Reduce motion Match device, Active, Inactive Match device High contrast Switch Inactive Underline links Switch Inactive Wider text spacing Switch Inactive An app's own choices After these (Starboard: Design) Done, Reset Buttons in the foot Choices apply at once. They are saved to the person's account where the app has accounts (the design task UX-29), otherwise in the browser. components.js applies them from data-display inputs. Every page uses Atkinson Hyperlegible Next, a typeface designed for readers with low vision, so there is no separate low-vision font switch. 2. Checks Structure - Check: one h1 per page; headings in order; landmarks ( header , nav , main ) on every page. - Check: a \"Skip to main content\" link is the first focusable element. - Check: the browser tab title names the page (\"Updates · Starboard\"). Keyboard and focus - Check: everything works with a keyboard alone, in a logical order. - Check: focus is always visible (3px outline at 3:1 contrast against both neighbours). - Check: the sticky header and save bar never cover what has focus (WCAG 2.4.11). The library's scroll-padding does this for .site-header and .save-bar ; never add a sticky part of your own. - Check: drawers and dialogs move focus in, trap it, return it to the opener on close, and close with Escape. Side panels (BookSummary, bookshelf drawer) follow the same rule. - Check: no keyboard trap anywhere else. - Check: no single-key shortcuts, or a way to deactivate them (WCAG 2.1.4). - Check: every drag has a way to do the same with buttons or the keyboard (WCAG 2.5.7). Size and spacing - Check: controls are at least "}, {"title": "WCAG 2.2 AA", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/wcag.html", "summary": "Accessibility and security guide", "text": " Version 1.0, 4 November 2026. This page shows how the TRex Design System meets WCAG 2.2 level AA (the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines), point by point. It follows the GOV.UK Service Manual's Understanding WCAG 2.2 . Every word follows CONTENT-DESIGN.md . About this guide What it is for WCAG is the international standard for accessible websites and apps. GOV.UK services must meet level AA, and so does everything in this design system. This guide says, for each thing the standard asks, what the library already does and what each app must still do on its own pages. The checks to run are in ACCESSIBILITY.md ; this guide says why each one is there. Who it is for Anyone who builds, reviews or tests a page of Starboard or Stallboard, or uses the design system for their own work. No knowledge of WCAG is needed: each point is explained in plain words, with its success criterion number for anyone who wants the full text. How to use it Read section 1 once. When you build a page, go through sections 2 to 5 and check each line marked \"The app\" for your page. Then run the checks in ACCESSIBILITY.md section 2 and the tests in its section 6. 1. What WCAG asks WCAG 2.2 has four principles. Content must be: perceivable: people can take it in with the senses they use, such as sight, hearing or touch; operable: people can use it however they work, such as with a keyboard, voice control or a switch; understandable: people can follow the content and predict how the page behaves; robust: browsers and assistive technology, such as screen readers, can read it reliably. Each principle has guidelines, and each guideline has success criteria at level A, AA or AAA. Level AA means meeting every A and AA criterion. WCAG helps people with sight, hearing, movement and thinking or understanding impairments, and helps everyone using a small screen, bright sunlight or a slow connection. In each table, \"The library\" is what tokens.css , components.css and components.js do for you, and \"The app\" is what each page must still do. 2. Perceivable What is needed Criterion The library The app A text alternative for every image and icon 1.1.1 Icons beside words are hidden from screen readers; icon-only buttons need a name; meters and charts carry their value in words Write each image's text description by ALT-TEXT.md Captions and transcripts for video and audio 1.2.1 to 1.2.5 Nothing: there is no video component Give any video captions, and any recorded speech a transcript Structure a screen re"}, {"title": "Security checks", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/security-checks.html", "summary": "Accessibility and security guide", "text": " Version 1.0, 3 November 2026. How Starboard and the other apps that use this design system check their security, sort what the checks find, and report the results. Every app runs the same checks and record them in the same way, so a person can compare them and trust what each one says. Every word follows CONTENT-DESIGN.md . About this guide What it is for A security check is only useful when someone acts on its results and writes them down. This guide lists the checks, says what each one finds and misses, and sets the rules for sorting findings and recording them. Who it is for Anyone who builds or releases Starboard or another app of the design system, including AI agents, and anyone who wants to know how far to trust them. No security background is needed: each check is explained from the start. How to use it Read section 1 for the checks at a glance. Before a release, run every check in sections 2 to 5 that the release touches. Section 7 says how each app runs them. Sort what they find with section 8. Record the results with section 9, and list the fixes in the release notes with section 10. Go through the checklist in section 11. The worked example is Starboard: its tools/security-scan.sh , tools/zap-rules.tsv and Security: how Starboard protects you, and how it was tested . For AI agents: treat every Rule as a check that must pass before a release is finished. Never mark a finding as not real without the evidence that section 8 asks for. 1. The checks at a glance Check What it finds When A pass is Unit and API tests A security rule in the code that has stopped working Every change Every test passes Live browser journeys Problems only a real browser shows: code run from data, a missing content policy, keys in a page, unsafe redirects Every push Every journey passes Trivy Known problems in the image, its libraries and the Dockerfile Every release 0 known problems, or each one explained OWASP ZAP Weaknesses in the running app, from outside Every release; the active scan when sign-in, requests or pages change No failures; each warning left is explained Gitleaks Keys and passwords anywhere in the git history Every commit (GitHub) and every release 0 findings, apart from made-up keys listed in .gitleaksignore Bandit and Semgrep Risky patterns in Python and in TypeScript or JavaScript Every release 0 medium or high findings, apart from lines marked with a reason ShellCheck Mistakes in shell scripts Every release 0 warnings Hadolint Mistakes in the Dockerfil"}, {"title": "Guides", "area": "Guides", "url": "guides/index.html", "summary": "All the guides.", "text": "Design system Component library Components by area Wizard Terminal Content design Help writing Changelogs Alt text Screenshots Accessibility WCAG 2.2 AA Security checks"}, {"title": "Foundations", "area": "Foundations", "url": "foundations/index.html", "summary": "Colour, accent colours, type, space and icons: the values every component uses.", "text": "Colour Role names for every colour, and how a theme changes the values. Accent colours The accent colours and the rules they keep in every theme. Type, space and icons One typeface, the size and space scales and the icon sizes."}, {"title": "Components", "area": "Components", "url": "components/index.html", "summary": "Every component of the shared library, live, with its markup and rules.", "text": "Shell The header and menus, the phone menu, page head, footer, Display, banners, alerts and the command palette. Actions Buttons and menu buttons. Inputs Fields, choices, filters, combobox, tag input, secret field, schedule and the error summary. Content Status, tooltips, facts, charts, rows, tables, notices, toasts, callouts, disclosure and the log viewer. Panels, cards and tabs Panels and cards, and tabs. Covers, media and shelves Covers and media cards, the A to Z list, load more, series header and shelf. Settings and toolbars The section toolbar, settings rows, the save bar, settings patterns, the folder picker, timeline and calendar. Slide-outs and dialogs The slide-out and the dialog. App patterns Starboard's own patterns: page topics, the update list, places and service logos, the dashboard, show once, a wizard in a slide-out and folding sections."}, {"title": "Patterns", "area": "Patterns", "url": "patterns/index.html", "summary": "How components work together: choosing, the wizard, the terminal, help and dates.", "text": "Choosing Slide-out, dialog or page, and which item to use. Wizard Every job done in steps: the pieces, and signing in to a service. Terminal How a wizard appears and behaves in a terminal. Lists and changes The event list, what will change, earlier and later, and recent checks. Help The help article, the help centre and what is new. Dates Retiring something, dated changes and time ago."}, {"title": "Home", "area": "Home", "url": "index.html", "summary": "The design system.", "text": ""}];
